---
import DetailHeading from "../detailHeading.astro";
---

<div class="space-y-8 text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
  <section>
    <DetailHeading>Problem</DetailHeading>
    <p class="mt-4">
      The collaborative board mode has to keep drawing and board state in sync for connected clients while accounts,
      boards and subscriptions remain part of the same product. The architecture addresses those concerns in one
      deployed application.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <DetailHeading>Responsibilities</DetailHeading>
    <p class="mt-4">
      I built the project end to end. The scope covers the Spring Boot API and Spring Security, the Next.js and
      Konva canvas experience, real-time updates, PostgreSQL and Redis data handling, Stripe billing, and the
      containerised delivery path.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <DetailHeading>Decisions and trade-offs</DetailHeading>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
      <li><span class="font-bold text-slate-300">WebSockets:</span> board updates move between connected clients in real time.</li>
      <li><span class="font-bold text-slate-300">PostgreSQL and Redis:</span> PostgreSQL stores boards and account data, while Redis supports fast, short-lived state.</li>
      <li><span class="font-bold text-slate-300">Konva:</span> the canvas renderer is kept in the frontend where drawing interactions happen.</li>
      <li><span class="font-bold text-slate-300">Delivery:</span> Docker, GitHub Actions and Google Cloud Run provide the build and hosting path; Stripe is integrated in the Spring Boot backend.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-500">
      The public project description does not publish latency, traffic or scale figures, so this case study keeps the
      result qualitative.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <DetailHeading>Technical architecture</DetailHeading>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
      <li><span class="font-bold text-slate-300">Backend:</span> Java 21, Spring Boot and Spring Security handle the API, authentication and authorisation.</li>
      <li><span class="font-bold text-slate-300">Frontend:</span> Next.js, React and TypeScript provide the UI, with Tailwind CSS and Konva for the canvas.</li>
      <li><span class="font-bold text-slate-300">Data:</span> PostgreSQL is the system of record for boards, accounts and related data; Redis is used for short-lived state.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-sm border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900 p-4 text-sm leading-tight text-slate-300"><code>[ Next.js / React / Konva ] &lt;--WebSocket--&gt; [ Spring Boot API ]
                                                    ↓
                                    [ PostgreSQL ]     [ Redis ]</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section>
    <DetailHeading>Result</DetailHeading>
    <p class="mt-4">
      The result is a live todrawn.com product with Scribe for hand-drawn video creation and collaborative boards,
      with Stripe subscriptions, a containerised application, automated GitHub Actions delivery and a backend hosted
      on Google Cloud Run.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <DetailHeading>Lessons learned</DetailHeading>
    <p class="mt-4">
      Building the product end to end connected frontend interactions, real-time transport, persistence,
      authentication, billing and deployment in one system. It also made the boundary between durable data in
      PostgreSQL and short-lived coordination in Redis explicit. The public material documents the architecture and
      production URL; it does not include a performance or incident history.
    </p>
  </section>
</div>
